Kent Paul Dolan wrote:
> Janis Papanagnou wrote:
> 
>  > With a standardized C++ language we're in a much
>  > better starting position than Rogue/Hack/Nethack
>  > had been at former times with C.
> 
> Modify that to say "with a standardized OOPL
> language" and I agree with you.

That's a valid generalization. But the context of my
answer was the thread chain about C++ being "the most
natural choice" [Doug]; and I agree with the posters.

(But actual language choice depends on which base we
would like to start development. _If_ starting from
scratch - which I think is a non-rewarding enduring
path to go - I see no problems using Java instead of
C++; or whatever other OO language fits.)

> I don't see C++ as
> an especially attractive choice, and my programming
> in C++ began way back in 1992. The language is an
> ugly set of compromises.

Besides the C compatibility issue (including size of
data types), what are you thinking of?

> [It's frankly astonishing that Stroustrup didn't get
> rid of this mess of non-standardized data sizes in
> the transition from C to C++.]

Yes. (Stroustrup commented on his available options
and decisions in some of his papers/books; I think
"The Design and Evolution of C++" might be his most
complete work on this topic.)

(It's ugly but I think this point is uncritical for
the purposes we're discussing here; having the whole
set of data abstraction available who cares whether
an int is 32 or 64 bit if you're using an application
domain object definition anyway. It's better to get
rid of that low-level programming issues early in the
design process.)

> There are also other useful standards available,
> like host-neutral data transmission formats that
> turn the byte orders around if necessary to have
> data streams all transmitted identically across
> a wire no matter the byte orders of the machines on
> each end.

Yes.

(I happen to be an ardent supporter of ASN.1 for that
purpose. Though, currently, people seem to prefer to
transmit bloated XML, and even use that data format
as "specification" as well.)

